But the hard constraint says "No em/en dashes, minus sign equivalents, double or spaced hyphens in ANY new string or comment". Hmm — "double or spaced hyphens in ANY new string or comment" — does that apply to CSS comments too? The existing CSS uses `-----` separators everywhere (e.g. `/* Plan blocks ------...------ */`). The repo convention treats CSS comment rules as exempt from double-hyphen (dashScan CSS rule only checks spaced hyphen + code points). The hard constraint's "double hyphens" for CSS comments... The established repo convention (dashScan CSS_FILES comment) says: "CSS is scanned too, with one exception: a custom property name begins with two hyphens, which is syntax rather than copy". It doesn't mention comment separators — and existing comments HAVE `-----` runs (which contain `--` double hyphens!). So CSS comment rules are clearly exempt in this repo's own scan. To be safe with BOTH readings though... the existing CSS is full of `/* X ----- */` and passes. If I avoid them entirely, no risk either way. But then my CSS would look inconsistent with the file. Hmm.

The acceptance check (verification #7) is a grep over planMath/planText for runway formulas, and dashScan for src files. The CSS scan = index.css for code points + spaced hyphen. Given the repo's own convention (dozens of `----` separators in index.css, passing the gate), I'll follow the file convention for separators but avoid spaced hyphens. Actually — wait, let me re-read the hard constraint: "No em/en dashes, minus sign equivalents, double or spaced hyphens in ANY new string or comment (numeric minus in formatted money allowed)."

